% A numbered, centred caption whose body is ordinary markdown, so [@citations]
% inside it are processed by citeproc. The Lua filter emits these around every
% markdown table caption; you can also write them by hand -- but the opening
% call and the caption text MUST share one markdown line:
%
%     \LabCaptionBegin{table} Data about the chemicals used [@palleros2000]
%     \LabCaptionEnd
%
% NEVER split that into "\LabCaptionBegin{table}" alone on its own line with
% the caption text starting on the next: pandoc's raw_tex extension parses a
% complete, balanced-brace macro call alone on a markdown line as its own
% RawBlock, and its LaTeX writer always inserts a blank line between distinct
% blocks -- which compiles to a real \par, splitting "Table N."/"Figure N."
% from the caption text onto two visibly separate lines. This exact comment
% used to show the broken two-line form (LR1-2, corrected here 2026-09-16
% after it recurred in a later orgchem report) -- CLAUDE.md \S7 has the full
% writeup and the `pandoc -t latex` test that confirms it either way.
\newcommand{\LabCaptionBegin}[1]{%
  \par\vspace{\LabCaptionSkip}\begingroup\refstepcounter{#1}%
  \footnotesize\itshape\centering
  \csname #1name\endcsname~\csname the#1\endcsname.\ \ignorespaces}
\newcommand{\LabCaptionEnd}{\par\endgroup\vspace{\LabCaptionSkip}}
% --- ir-spectra
% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
% 15. IR spectra — native vector figures read straight from CSV
%
%     Replaces the old workflow (matplotlib PNG/SVG built outside LaTeX) for
%     the ordinary case, single spectrum AND comparison alike: pgfplots reads
%     the CSV pair produced by the .SPA parser documented in
%     SPA_PROCESSING_INSTRUCTIONS.md directly, no external plotting step.
%       - the ARCHIVAL csv (semicolon, decimal comma) is the author's own export,
%         full range, untouched by anything here;
%       - the PLAIN csv (comma, dot-decimal, header "wavenumber,intensity")
%         is what these macros read, and is clipped to >=650 cm^-1 at write
%         time (the iS5's detector noise below that is never plotted, per the
%         house rule — see SPA_PROCESSING_INSTRUCTIONS.md sec. 4).
%       - the y-axis label (Transmittance % / Absorbance) is an explicit
%         argument here, not a heuristic this package runs: the .SPA parser
%         guesses a sensible default when it writes the CSV, but the report
%         author sees and can override the guess right here in the call,
%         rather than only by re-running the parsing script.
%
%     Peak coordinates are supplied by whoever built the CSV, not looked up
%     here: the same Python step that identifies a peak already has the
%     array in memory, so it emits the numbers once, verified, rather than
%     this package re-deriving y from x via a fragile nearest-neighbour
%     search over a few thousand unevenly-visited TeX comparisons. This is
%     the same "verify with code" rule the rest of the package already runs
%     on every calculation (INSTRUCTIONS_for_future_claude_LR.md sec. 8).
%     Concretely: whatever future session identifies a peak from the raw
%     .SPA data for the Discussion is the SAME script step that emits that
%     peak's x/label_y pair for the figure call below — one source of truth,
%     so a peak discussed in prose is never left unlabelled on its own figure.
%
%     Peak labels sit BELOW the trough, between the curve and the x-axis —
%     the OMNIC/Spectragryph/literature convention — with a short grey
%     leader tick, rotated 90 degrees. Each peak is given as x/label_y —
%     the wavenumber and the label's already-chosen y-position (i.e.
%     trough_y minus whatever gap avoids the neighbours), not x/y/offset:
%     see \Lab@irpeaklist below for why, and for the real, reproduced
%     pgfplots bug that ruled out both \foreach and \pgfplotsforeachungrouped
%     for firing that loop. Collisions in the fingerprint region are handled
%     the same way the rest of the package handles a stubborn layout: render,
%     look at the PNG, nudge the number, rerun — not automatic collision
%     avoidance.
%
%     Choosing label_y itself is the part worth doing by measurement rather
%     than by eye (found wrong on LR1-2 p12 fig. 4, 2026-09-09: several
%     fingerprint-region labels sat 10-22 units below their own trough, with
%     the short 3-unit tick left dangling in blank space well short of the
%     curve, while the SAME figure's other labels — chosen closer to their
%     troughs — read fine). The reliable recipe: read the actual CSV, take
%     the minimum y within a small window of the target wavenumber (+/-15
%     cm-1 is plenty), and set label_y to that trough minus about 5-8 units
%     — enough for the tick to visibly reach the curve without the label
%     text touching it. Only stagger a label further below that floor when
%     two peaks sit close enough in x for their (vertically rotated) label
%     text to collide otherwise, as the existing carbonyl/C-H cluster above
%     already does.
%
%     Sizing that gap: keep it SMALL by default — label_y = trough_y minus
%     roughly 5 (just past the 3-unit tick, plus a sliver of clearance) —
%     the label should read as sitting just under its own trough, not
%     floating halfway down the axis. Only push a label further down, in
%     increments of about 5 more, when its wavenumber is close enough to a
%     neighbour that their rotated text would otherwise overlap (the
%     fingerprint region, mostly); an isolated peak never needs more than
%     the default ~5. Confirmed by eye on real data — the author flagged a first
%     pass that used gaps of 10-26 as "far below the line".
%
%     Because peak labels are placed BELOW the data minimum by an amount
%     that has nothing to do with the data's own range, pgfplots' automatic
%     y-scaling (enlarge y limits) generally will NOT leave enough headroom
%     for the lowest label once a real spectrum's fingerprint region is
%     labelled — this was confirmed while prototyping, not a guess. Pass an
%     explicit ymin low enough for the lowest label_y in the call, e.g.
%     \irspectrum[ymin=30]{...}: this is the same "explicit, overridable"
%     approach as the y-axis label above, decided once by whoever built the
%     peak list rather than guessed silently in here.
%
%     Scaling: width/height are ordinary pgfplots options, overridable via
%     the optional argument, so the SAME macro serves a normal inline
%     portrait figure and a full-magnification figure inside \landscape
%     (\irspectrum[width=\linewidth,height=0.62\linewidth]{...} typically
%     wants a wider aspect than the portrait default) — see manual sec. 4.5
%     for the [H]-inside-landscape rule, which still applies here.
%
%     \irspectrumcompare stacks two spectra in independent panels with their
%     OWN y-scales (a groupplots column), rather than overlaying them on one
%     shared axis: real reports here have one trace bottoming out around
%     44 %T against another sitting on a 95 % baseline, and overlaid on one
%     axis the weaker trace reads as noise. The depth difference is itself
%     part of the finding, so each panel is scaled to show its own trace
%     properly, not squashed to share the other's range.
% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
\RequirePackage{pgfplots}
\RequirePackage{tikz}
\usetikzlibrary{pgfplots.groupplots}
% positioning: `below=of <node>` placement, as in the prelab flowcharts
% (LR6 prelab6). Without it TikZ parses `of n1` as maths and stops with
% "PGF Math Error: Unknown function `of'".
\usetikzlibrary{positioning}
\pgfplotsset{compat=1.18}
\definecolor{nuirred}{HTML}{B03A2E}  % SPA_PROCESSING_INSTRUCTIONS.md sec. 5 house colour

% \irspectrumcompare's [tbp] change (immediately below) fixed a real
% mid-text blank-hole bug (see docs/CHANGELOG.md v1.14.3) but exposed a
% SEPARATE, standard LaTeX default: a page made up ENTIRELY of float
% material (nothing else fits beside a ~half-page-tall \irspectrumcompare,
% so it lands alone) is, by plain LaTeX kernel default, VERTICALLY CENTRED
% on that page -- \@fptop/\@fpsep/\@fpbot (the skip glue inserted
% above/between/below floats on such a page) are all `0pt plus 1fil` out
% of the box, i.e. equal infinite stretch on both sides. Found on LR3,
% 2026-09-18, immediately after the [H]->[tbp] change: the figure read as
% centred with ~161pt above it and ~170pt below, not pinned to the top
% with slack only at the bottom as expected. Confirmed independent of
% \flushbottom/\raggedbottom (identical pixel measurements under both --
% this is the float-page mechanism, not the page-filling one \S1's
% \flushbottom comment documents; do not conflate the two). Fix is the
% standard one (LaTeX Companion / comp.text.tex FAQ): zero the TOP skip so
% floats start right at the top of a float-only page, and leave the
% BOTTOM skip stretchable so leftover space collects only below --
% \@fptop/\@fpbot are skip REGISTERS (\newskip in the kernel), set with
% \setlength, NOT macros -- a first attempt with \renewcommand faulted
% "Missing number, treated as zero." Verified by direct pixel
% measurement, not assumed: the ~161pt leading gap above the figure on its
% own page dropped to ~10pt (ordinary top margin) after this, with the
% full leftover collecting below the caption as expected.
\makeatletter
\setlength{\@fptop}{0pt}
\setlength{\@fpbot}{0pt plus 1fil}
\makeatother

% @-names below need catcode 11 for @: automatic while nulabreport.sty itself
% loads (package files get this for free), but this snippet is also \input
% directly for prototyping, where it is NOT automatic — hence the explicit
% \makeatletter/\makeatother, harmless either way.
\makeatletter

% Fixed leader-tick length, in y-axis data units (Transmittance %/Absorbance).
% A short tick pointing up from the label toward the curve reads the same as
% Spectragryph's own ticks without needing the label's exact vertical gap
% from the trough (that gap is a free per-peak choice, made in Python).
\newcommand{\Lab@irtickval}{3}

% Colours of the trace and its labels (v1.25.0). Three macros, set once per
% trace by \Lab@pickcolors (below) and read by whatever draws it:
%   \Lab@tc        the trace colour (the \addplot line)
%   \Lab@tickcol   the leader tick of every label
%   \Lab@labkeys   extra node keys for the label text: empty, or text=<colour>
% The defaults here are what every figure drew before v1.25.0 -- a nuirred
% line, grey ticks, labels in the document's own text colour -- so a call that
% names no colour renders exactly as it did (pixel-diffed, CHANGELOG v1.25.0).
% A label colour, when given, colours the text AND its tick: a red label on
% a grey tick next to a red line reads as two things.
%
% THESE MUST BE EXPANDED WHERE THEY ARE USED (\expanded below), never left
% as macro names inside a \draw/\node/\addplot. pgfplots postpones all of
% those until \end{axis}; a macro name is then read AFTER the last trace was
% set up, so on an overlay every trace and label came out in the LAST trace's
% colour (found building the overlay: two traces, both red). And a macro in
% a pgfkeys list is read as one key name, so \Lab@labkeys cannot be a
% bare entry either -- expanded it is the two tokens text=<colour>.
\newcommand{\Lab@tc}{nuirred}
\newcommand{\Lab@tickcol}{gray!65}
\newcommand{\Lab@labkeys}{}

% Internal: one peak label + leader tick, placed BELOW the trough.
% #1 = x (wavenumber), #2 = label_y (the label's chosen y-position, already
% below the trough by whatever gap avoids its neighbours)
\newcommand{\Lab@irpeak}[2]{%
  \expanded{\noexpand\draw[\Lab@tickcol, thin]}
    (axis cs:#1,#2) -- (axis cs:#1,#2+\Lab@irtickval);
  \expanded{\noexpand\node[rotate=90, anchor=east, font=\noexpand\scriptsize,
        inner sep=1pt, fill=white, fill opacity=0.85, text opacity=1,
        \Lab@labkeys]}
    at (axis cs:#1,#2) {#1};
}

% Fires \Lab@irpeak per x/label_y pair. Deliberately NOT \foreach and NOT
% \pgfplotsforeachungrouped: both were tried and both are broken for this
% exact job, reproduced in isolation while prototyping, not hypothetical:
%   - plain \foreach (pgffor) opens a TeX group per iteration, which
%     collides with the axis/tikzpicture environment's own group bookkeeping
%     and throws "Missing \endgroup inserted" / "ended by \end{document}"
%     past the first iteration (also true of LaTeX's \@for — same cause,
%     since it also groups its loop body).
%   - \pgfplotsforeachungrouped avoids THAT error — every iteration's macro
%     body genuinely runs (confirmed with \typeout) — but something in its
%     own "ungrouped" replay mechanism (\pgfplotsapplistXX* feeding
%     \pgfplotsforeachentryinCSVisterminated) discards every \fill/\node
%     issued before the LAST iteration: only the final peak ever reaches the
%     page, silently, with no error and no warning. Reproduced with plain
%     \fill circles (no \node, no rotation, no anchor) on TeX Live 2023's
%     pgfplots, so it is a property of that replay mechanism itself, not a
%     node-anchoring or coordinate-system quirk.
% The fix is a hand-rolled, ungrouped, purely-recursive delimited-argument
% loop: no \begingroup/\endgroup anywhere, so nothing for the axis to choke
% on, and no dependence on pgfplots' own (buggy, for this use) replay queue.
% Terminates on a \relax/\relax sentinel appended to the list at call time.
% Guards the empty-list case (a spectrum with nothing worth labelling):
% without this, an empty #1 feeds the delimited scanner a bare comma before
% it expects one and mis-splits the sentinel itself.
\def\Lab@irpeaklist#1{%
  \def\Lab@irpeaklist@chk{#1}%
  \ifx\Lab@irpeaklist@chk\@empty
  \else
    \Lab@irpeaklist@i#1,\relax/\relax,%
  \fi
}
\def\Lab@irpeaklist@i#1/#2,{%
  \ifx\relax#1%
  \else
    \Lab@irpeak{#1}{#2}%
    \expandafter\Lab@irpeaklist@i
  \fi
}

% Apex labels (v1.19.0) -- the other label style, for data whose features
% point UP (chromatograms). One entry per peak:
%     x/apex_y/label_y/name
% A horizontal two-line label (name over x) whose bottom edge sits at
% label_y, joined to the peak by a thin leader from just above apex_y. The
% leader is what keeps a label that had to be raised clear of a neighbour
% (a crowded cluster) attached to its own peak; for an uncrowded peak it is
% only a short tick, the same visual as \Lab@irpeak's. Empty name: x alone.
% label_y is chosen by the script that found the peak (gc_tool.py
% integrate), never by this macro -- the same division of labour as the
% trough labels above. Wrap a name containing a comma or slash in braces.
\newcommand{\Lab@apexpeak}[4]{%
  \expanded{\noexpand\draw[\Lab@tickcol, thin]}
    ([yshift=1.5pt]axis cs:#1,#2) -- (axis cs:#1,#3);
  \expanded{\noexpand\node[anchor=south, font=\noexpand\scriptsize, align=center,
        inner sep=1pt, fill=white, fill opacity=0.85, text opacity=1,
        \Lab@labkeys]}
    at (axis cs:#1,#3) {\ifblank{#4}{#1}{#4\\#1}};
}
% Same hand-rolled ungrouped recursion as \Lab@irpeaklist, for the same
% reasons (see the comment above it) -- four fields instead of two.
\def\Lab@apexpeaklist#1{%
  \def\Lab@apexpeaklist@chk{#1}%
  \ifx\Lab@apexpeaklist@chk\@empty
  \else
    \Lab@apexpeaklist@i#1,\relax/\relax/\relax/\relax,%
  \fi
}
\def\Lab@apexpeaklist@i#1/#2/#3/#4,{%
  \ifx\relax#1%
  \else
    \Lab@apexpeak{#1}{#2}{#3}{#4}%
    \expandafter\Lab@apexpeaklist@i
  \fi
}
% Fanned labels (v1.28.0) -- the third label style, for NMR, where lines
% stand a fraction of a millimetre apart (methyl cinnamate's 130.5, 129.1,
% 128.3 ppm are 1.6 mm apart on a 14.5 cm, 200 ppm axis; even a rotated
% label is 2.6 mm across). The labels are spread along the top, as a
% spectrometer prints them, each joined to its line by a leader that rises
% from the line, bends at the knee, and runs to the label. One entry:
%     x/apex_y/knee_y/label_x/label_y/text
% Everything except the drawing is decided by the script that found the
% peaks (spectrum_digitize.py nmr), never here -- the same division of
% labour as the other two styles. Empty text: x itself.
% Empty knee_y (v1.28.1): a callout instead, as the book marks its solvent
% line ("solvent" with an arrow): an arrow from (label_x, label_y) to the
% line's top and the text upright, centred above the arrow's tail, so it
% reads the same whichever side of the line the script put it.
\newcommand{\Lab@fanpeak}[6]{%
  \ifblank{#3}{%
    \expanded{\noexpand\draw[\Lab@tickcol, thin, ->]}
      (axis cs:#4,#5) -- ([yshift=1.5pt]axis cs:#1,#2);
    \expanded{\noexpand\node[anchor=south, font=\noexpand\scriptsize,
          inner sep=1pt, \Lab@labkeys]}
      at (axis cs:#4,#5) {\ifblank{#6}{#1}{#6}};
  }{%
    \expanded{\noexpand\draw[\Lab@tickcol, thin]}
      ([yshift=1.5pt]axis cs:#1,#2) -- (axis cs:#1,#3) -- (axis cs:#4,#5);
    \expanded{\noexpand\node[rotate=90, anchor=west, font=\noexpand\scriptsize,
          inner sep=1pt, \Lab@labkeys]}
      at (axis cs:#4,#5) {\ifblank{#6}{#1}{#6}};
  }%
}
% Same hand-rolled ungrouped recursion as \Lab@irpeaklist (see the comment
% above it for why neither \foreach nor \pgfplotsforeachungrouped will do).
\def\Lab@fanpeaklist#1{%
  \def\Lab@fanpeaklist@chk{#1}%
  \ifx\Lab@fanpeaklist@chk\@empty
  \else
    \Lab@fanpeaklist@i#1,\relax/\relax/\relax/\relax/\relax/\relax,%
  \fi
}
\def\Lab@fanpeaklist@i#1/#2/#3/#4/#5/#6,{%
  \ifx\relax#1%
  \else
    \Lab@fanpeak{#1}{#2}{#3}{#4}{#5}{#6}%
    \expandafter\Lab@fanpeaklist@i
  \fi
}
\let\Lab@peaks@trough\Lab@irpeaklist
\let\Lab@peaks@apex\Lab@apexpeaklist
\let\Lab@peaks@fan\Lab@fanpeaklist

% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
% Plot kinds (v1.19.0). Everything \irspectrum/\irspectrumstack used to
% hardcode that is specific to IR -- the axis defaults, the CSV's column
% names, and which way peak labels hang -- is now one named "kind", and the
% float/stack/placement machinery below is shared by every kind. Asked for
% when the GC tool was written: "can we make that kind of feature
% universal? not only ir specific". A new kind of data (UV-Vis, HPLC, ...)
% is one \labdefineplotkind line, in a report's preamble or here:
%
%   \labdefineplotkind{name}{x column}{y column}{trough|apex|fan}{axis keys}
%
% `trough`: x/label_y lists, rotated labels hanging below (IR, %T).
% `apex`:   x/apex_y/label_y/name lists, labels above (chromatograms, MS).
% `fan`:    x/apex_y/knee_y/label_x/label_y/text lists, rotated labels
%           spread along the top on bent leaders (NMR; v1.28.0).
% Axis keys are ordinary pgfplots keys; a call's own options come after
% them, so any default is overridable per figure.
% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
\newcommand{\labdefineplotkind}[5]{%
  \pgfplotsset{nulab kind #1/.style={#5},
               nulab kind #1 table/.style={x=#2,y=#3},
               nulab kind #1 stack/.style={}}%
  \expandafter\let\csname Lab@kind@#1@peaks\expandafter\endcsname
    \csname Lab@peaks@#4\endcsname
}
% ir: the exact keys \irspectrum/\irspectrumstack hardcoded up to v1.18.1
% (moved here unchanged; the pixel diff in docs/CHANGELOG.md v1.19.0
% confirms they still render identically).
\labdefineplotkind{ir}{wavenumber}{intensity}{trough}{%
  x dir=reverse, xmin=650, xmax=4000,
  enlarge y limits={upper=0.08,lower=0.15},
  xlabel={Wavenumber / cm\textsuperscript{-1}},
  axis lines=box, tick align=outside,
  xtick={4000,3500,3000,2500,2000,1500,1000},
  minor x tick num=4,
}
% Extra keys for a kind when it is drawn as a stack (\labstack and
% everything built on it), empty unless a kind sets them (v1.20.0).
% ir: every panel's y label at ONE fixed distance from the axis line. By
% default pgfplots places it just outside that panel's own widest tick label,
% so a panel whose top tick reads "100" had its "Transmittance %" further
% left than one topping out at "80" -- a visibly ragged column of labels
% down the stack (the author, LR4-5 review). The fixed offset is the
% default position for a "100" panel (outside tick length + the tick
% label's two inner seps + the width of "100"), so those labels stay put and
% the others move out to meet them. Singles (\irspectrum) are unchanged.
% The shift is `yshift`, not `xshift`: the label is already rotated 90
% degrees by then, and its own y axis points away from the plot (an xshift
% slid it along the axis instead, onto the tick labels).
\pgfplotsset{nulab kind ir stack/.style={
  every axis y label/.append style={
    at={(rel axis cs:0,0.5)}, anchor=south,
    yshift={\pgfkeysvalueof{/pgfplots/major tick length}+0.6667em+width("100")}},
}}
% gc: gc_tool.py's plain CSV (time,signal). The raw FID signal sits on a
% ~600 000-count offset, so tick labels are fixed-point with thin-space
% thousands (600 000, not pgfplots' default "6 . 10^5" rescaling, which
% reads badly when the whole plot spans 6-11 x 10^5). `unbounded coords=
% jump` makes the nan rows `gc_tool.py crop --exclude` writes into a GAP
% in the trace, not a line drawn across the removed region. No x padding:
% the run's own start/end, or xmin/xmax for a crop, are the edges.
% `clip mode=individual`: clip the TRACE to the axis box, not the labels --
% with the default (global) clip, a peak label near xmin/xmax had its text
% cut off at the frame (found on an axis-cropped group 1, "Methanol" at
% xmin=2.6 printed as "lethanol").
\labdefineplotkind{gc}{time}{signal}{apex}{%
  clip mode=individual,
  xlabel={Retention time / min},
  ylabel={Signal / counts},
  axis lines=box, tick align=outside,
  enlarge x limits=false,
  enlarge y limits={upper=0.02,lower=0.04},
  minor x tick num=1,
  unbounded coords=jump,
  scaled y ticks=false,
  yticklabel style={/pgf/number format/fixed, /pgf/number format/1000 sep={\,}},
}
% titration: a CSV-driven volume/pH curve (v1.22.0) -- the shape a real
% CHEM220L QCA report was hand-rolling as a raw tikzpicture/axis pair
% instead of a plot kind (found scanning real reports for the v1.21.2
% manual/examples pass; added once the author confirmed it separately, not
% folded into that docs-only pass). `apex` labelling reads naturally here
% for the equivalence point (a label above the curve), even though a
% titration curve has no real trough/apex peaks of its own. ymin/ymax fixed
% to the full pH scale by default, since 0-14 is the meaningful range
% regardless of what one run's own data spans -- override per call
% (`\labplot[kind=titration, ymax=...]`) for a non-aqueous or narrower-range
% titration.
\labdefineplotkind{titration}{volume}{ph}{apex}{%
  xlabel={Volume of titrant / mL}, ylabel={pH},
  axis lines=box, tick align=outside,
  enlarge x limits=false, ymin=0, ymax=14,
}
% ms (v1.28.0): a stick mass spectrum, spectrum_digitize.py ms's CSV
% (mz,intensity: one row per stick, relative abundance in %). The sticks are
% the kind's own business: `ycomb` on every plot of the axis draws each row
% as a line from 0 up to its value, so the shared drawing code (\Lab@plotbody,
% the stacks) is untouched. Labels: the `apex` style, m/z alone (empty name).
% ymin=0 fixed: a stick stands on the axis.
\labdefineplotkind{ms}{mz}{intensity}{apex}{%
  every axis plot post/.append style={ycomb, line cap=butt},
  xlabel={\textit{m/z}}, ylabel={Relative abundance / \%},
  axis lines=box, tick align=outside,
  ymin=0, enlarge y limits={upper=0.10},
  enlarge x limits=0.03,
  minor x tick num=1,
  clip mode=individual,
}
% nmr (v1.28.0): a 1H or 13C spectrum, spectrum_digitize.py nmr's CSV
% (shift,intensity; intensity in % of the trace's tallest point -- only
% relative heights mean anything in a digitized NMR figure, so there is no y
% axis, as on the spectrometer's own plots). Shift runs right to left.
% Labels: the `fan` style (\Lab@fanpeak). clip mode=individual for the same
% reason as gc: a label near xmin/xmax must not be cut at the frame.
\labdefineplotkind{nmr}{shift}{intensity}{fan}{%
  x dir=reverse,
  xlabel={$\delta$ / ppm},
  axis x line*=bottom, axis y line=none, ytick=\empty,
  tick align=outside,
  enlarge x limits=false,
  enlarge y limits={upper=0.05,lower=0.01},
  minor x tick num=1,
  clip mode=individual,
}
% xsize / ysize (v1.28.4): a plot's width / height as a fraction of what it
% would otherwise be -- the 14.5 x 9.5 cm default, or the call's own width=/
% height= -- e.g. \labplot[kind=ms, xsize=0.9, ysize=0.3]: 90 % as wide, 30 %
% as tall. Asked for as "y-30% x-90%"; a % cannot be typed (it starts a TeX
% comment), so a fraction. Short names for pgfplots' own x/y post scale,
% which scale the axis after its size is worked out (the tick labels and
% text stay their size), the same for every kind and for stacks, overlays
% and insets. Unset, nothing changes. A size relative to the page is
% pgfplots' own width=0.9\linewidth, height=0.3\textheight.
\pgfplotsset{
  xsize/.style={x post scale=#1},
  ysize/.style={y post scale=#1},
}

% Split `kind=` (alias `technique=`, what \chromatogram documents) out of a
% call's option list; everything else passes through to pgfplots verbatim.
% l3keys, because it can hand back the keys it doesn't know, which pgfkeys'
% own .unknown handlers cannot do cleanly inside an option list meant for a
% later \begin{axis}. Then calls #2{kind}{remaining keys}.
\ExplSyntaxOn
\tl_new:N \l__nulab_plot_kind_tl
\tl_new:N \l__nulab_plot_rest_tl
\tl_new:N \l__nulab_plot_tcol_tl
\tl_new:N \l__nulab_plot_lcol_tl
\clist_new:N \l__nulab_plot_ovcols_clist
\keys_define:nn { nulab/plot }
  {
    kind      .tl_set:N = \l__nulab_plot_kind_tl ,
    technique .tl_set:N = \l__nulab_plot_kind_tl ,
    % v1.25.0: colours. `trace color` is the line (default nuirred),
    % `label color` the peak labels and their ticks (default: unchanged);
    % `overlay colors` the palette an overlay hands out, one entry per
    % trace in call order (default black, nuirred -- the reference report's
    % pair). All three are taken out here so pgfplots never sees them.
    trace~color    .tl_set:N    = \l__nulab_plot_tcol_tl ,
    label~color    .tl_set:N    = \l__nulab_plot_lcol_tl ,
    overlay~colors .clist_set:N = \l__nulab_plot_ovcols_clist ,
    % v1.28.2: inset={...}, repeatable, \labplot only (see \Lab@insets below)
    inset          .code:n      = \tl_put_right:Nn \l__nulab_plot_insets_tl { {#1} } ,
    % v1.28.4: integrals={csv}, \labplot only: a 1H figure's integral curves
    % (spectrum_digitize.py's _integrals CSV), drawn over the trace
    integrals      .tl_set:N    = \l__nulab_plot_integrals_tl ,
  }
\tl_new:N \l__nulab_plot_insets_tl
\cs_new_protected:Npn \Lab@plotsplit #1 #2
  {
    \tl_clear:N \l__nulab_plot_kind_tl
    \tl_clear:N \l__nulab_plot_tcol_tl
    \tl_clear:N \l__nulab_plot_lcol_tl
    \tl_clear:N \l__nulab_plot_insets_tl
    \tl_clear:N \l__nulab_plot_integrals_tl
    \clist_set:Nn \l__nulab_plot_ovcols_clist { black , nuirred }
    \keys_set_known:nnN { nulab/plot } {#1} \l__nulab_plot_rest_tl
    \tl_if_blank:VT \l__nulab_plot_kind_tl
      { \msg_error:nn { nulabreport } { plot-no-kind } }
    \cs_if_exist:cF { Lab@kind@ \l__nulab_plot_kind_tl @peaks }
      {
        \msg_error:nnV { nulabreport } { plot-unknown-kind } \l__nulab_plot_kind_tl
        \tl_set:Nn \l__nulab_plot_kind_tl { ir }
      }
    \use:e
      {
        \exp_not:n {#2}
        { \exp_not:V \l__nulab_plot_kind_tl }
        { \exp_not:V \l__nulab_plot_rest_tl }
      }
  }
\msg_new:nnn { nulabreport } { plot-no-kind }
  { \token_to_str:N \labplot / \token_to_str:N \labstack ~ need ~ kind=... ~
    (ir, ~ gc, ~ or ~ one ~ made ~ with ~ \token_to_str:N \labdefineplotkind). }
\msg_new:nnn { nulabreport } { plot-unknown-kind }
  { Unknown ~ plot ~ kind ~ `#1'. ~ Define ~ it ~ first: ~
    \token_to_str:N \labdefineplotkind {#1}{x ~ column}{y ~ column}{trough|apex|fan}{axis ~ keys}. }

% Per-panel colours (v1.25.0): the same two keys, taken out of ONE panel's
% option list ( \labpanel{csv}{panel options}{peaks} ), so a stack panel or
% an overlay trace can override the call-level choice. A key the panel does
% not set stays blank, which \Lab@pickcolors reads as "fall back".
% GLOBAL assignments: in a stack, \nextgroupplot (which a panel calls after
% its keys are split) closes the previous panel's TeX group, and a local
% value set before it was reverted to the previous panel's -- panel 2
% silently inherited panel 1's colours.
\tl_new:N \l__nulab_panel_tcol_tl
\tl_new:N \l__nulab_panel_lcol_tl
\tl_new:N \l__nulab_panel_rest_tl
\tl_new:N \l__nulab_pick_l_tl
\keys_define:nn { nulab/panel }
  {
    trace~color .tl_gset:N = \l__nulab_panel_tcol_tl ,
    label~color .tl_gset:N = \l__nulab_panel_lcol_tl ,
  }
% \Lab@panelsplit{panel options}{callback}: calls callback{remaining keys},
% with the panel's own colours left in \l__nulab_panel_{t,l}col_tl.
\cs_new_protected:Npn \Lab@panelsplit #1 #2
  {
    \tl_gclear:N \l__nulab_panel_tcol_tl
    \tl_gclear:N \l__nulab_panel_lcol_tl
    \keys_set_known:nnN { nulab/panel } {#1} \l__nulab_panel_rest_tl
    \use:e
      {
        \exp_not:n {#2}
        { \exp_not:V \l__nulab_panel_rest_tl }
      }
  }
% Insets (v1.28.2): an expansion drawn INSIDE a \labplot, where the book's
% figure has it (an NMR figure's zoomed boxes). Each `inset={...}` key of the
% call is one inset:
%     inset={csv=file.csv, box={left,bottom,right,top}, peaks={...},
%            options={xmin=..., xmax=..., ymin=..., ymax=..., xtick={...}}}
% box: the corners of the inset's plot rectangle in the MAIN axis' own
% coordinates (x as the main axis reads it, so 8.8,...,5.0,... on a reversed
% ppm axis), which spectrum_digitize.py takes from the figure; options: the
% inset axis' own pgfplots keys (its x and y range). The inset is a second
% axis of the same kind, laid on the plot rectangle the box spans (scale
% only axis), with no x label and \scriptsize tick labels; peaks as the
% kind's labels. The corners are written into the main axis as literal
% numbers (pgfplots replays an axis' coordinates at \end{axis}, after a
% macro holding them may have moved on), with a catcode-12 colon: under
% \ExplSyntaxOn `:` is a letter and TikZ does not see `axis cs:` in it
% ("Unknown function `axis'"). The inset axes are drawn after the main one.
% \labplot only: a \labstack given inset= warns (\Lab@labstack@i).
\tl_new:N \l__nulab_in_csv_tl
\tl_new:N \l__nulab_in_peaks_tl
\tl_new:N \l__nulab_in_box_tl
\tl_new:N \l__nulab_in_opts_tl
\clist_new:N \l__nulab_in_box_clist
\int_new:N \l__nulab_in_int
\tl_new:N \Lab@inmarks
\tl_new:N \Lab@inaxes
\tl_new:N \Lab@inintegrals
\keys_define:nn { nulab/inset }
  {
    csv     .tl_set:N = \l__nulab_in_csv_tl ,
    peaks   .tl_set:N = \l__nulab_in_peaks_tl ,
    box     .tl_set:N = \l__nulab_in_box_tl ,
    options .tl_set:N = \l__nulab_in_opts_tl ,
  }
% \Lab@insets@build{kind}: \Lab@inmarks (the corner coordinates, for inside
% the main axis) and \Lab@inaxes (the inset axes, for after it) from the
% call's inset= keys; both empty without any, so a plot without insets is
% drawn exactly as before.
\cs_new_protected:Npn \Lab@insets@build #1
  {
    \tl_clear:N \Lab@inmarks
    \tl_clear:N \Lab@inaxes
    % v1.28.4: the integrals= curves, black over the trace as the book draws
    % them; one CSV, the curves separated by nan rows (unbounded coords=jump
    % makes each a gap). The file name is expanded in now, as the corners are.
    \tl_clear:N \Lab@inintegrals
    \tl_if_blank:VF \l__nulab_plot_integrals_tl
      {
        \tl_set:Nx \Lab@inintegrals
          {
            \exp_not:N \addplot [ black , line~join=round , unbounded~coords=jump , forget~plot ]
              table [ nulab~kind~#1~table , col~sep=comma ] { \exp_not:V \l__nulab_plot_integrals_tl } ;
          }
      }
    \int_zero:N \l__nulab_in_int
    \tl_map_inline:Nn \l__nulab_plot_insets_tl
      {
        \int_incr:N \l__nulab_in_int
        \tl_clear:N \l__nulab_in_csv_tl
        \tl_clear:N \l__nulab_in_peaks_tl
        \tl_clear:N \l__nulab_in_box_tl
        \tl_clear:N \l__nulab_in_opts_tl
        \keys_set:nn { nulab/inset } {##1}
        \clist_set:NV \l__nulab_in_box_clist \l__nulab_in_box_tl
        \int_compare:nNnTF { \clist_count:N \l__nulab_in_box_clist } = { 4 }
          {
            \tl_put_right:Nx \Lab@inmarks
              {
                \exp_not:N \coordinate ( Lab@in \int_use:N \l__nulab_in_int sw ) ~ at ~
                  ( axis~cs \c_colon_str \clist_item:Nn \l__nulab_in_box_clist { 1 } ,
                             \clist_item:Nn \l__nulab_in_box_clist { 2 } ) ;
                \exp_not:N \coordinate ( Lab@in \int_use:N \l__nulab_in_int ne ) ~ at ~
                  ( axis~cs \c_colon_str \clist_item:Nn \l__nulab_in_box_clist { 3 } ,
                             \clist_item:Nn \l__nulab_in_box_clist { 4 } ) ;
              }
            \tl_put_right:Nx \Lab@inaxes
              {
                \exp_not:N \Lab@insetaxis { #1 } { \int_use:N \l__nulab_in_int }
                  { \exp_not:V \l__nulab_in_csv_tl }
                  { \exp_not:V \l__nulab_in_opts_tl }
                  { \exp_not:V \l__nulab_in_peaks_tl }
              }
          }
          { \msg_error:nnx { nulabreport } { inset-box } { \int_use:N \l__nulab_in_int } }
      }
  }
\msg_new:nnn { nulabreport } { inset-box }
  { inset ~ #1 ~ needs ~ box={left,bottom,right,top} ~ (four ~ numbers, ~ the ~ main ~ axis' ~
    coordinates); ~ it ~ is ~ left ~ out. }
\msg_new:nnn { nulabreport } { inset-stack }
  { #1= ~ is ~ for ~ \token_to_str:N \labplot \c_space_tl only; ~ a ~
    \token_to_str:N \labstack \c_space_tl ignores ~ it. }
% \Lab@pickcolors{default trace colour}{labels follow the trace? true|false}
% sets \Lab@tc, \Lab@tickcol and \Lab@labkeys for the panel about to be drawn.
%   trace  = the panel's `trace color`, else the default passed in;
%   labels = the panel's `label color`, else the call's, else (if the second
%            argument is true) the trace's own colour, else the unchanged
%            look. An overlay passes true: its labels match their line.
\cs_new_protected:Npn \Lab@pickcolors #1 #2
  {
    \tl_set_eq:NN \Lab@tc \l__nulab_panel_tcol_tl
    \tl_if_blank:VT \Lab@tc { \tl_set:Nn \Lab@tc {#1} }
    \tl_set_eq:NN \l__nulab_pick_l_tl \l__nulab_panel_lcol_tl
    \tl_if_blank:VT \l__nulab_pick_l_tl
      { \tl_set_eq:NN \l__nulab_pick_l_tl \l__nulab_plot_lcol_tl }
    \bool_if:nT {#2}
      {
        \tl_if_blank:VT \l__nulab_pick_l_tl
          { \tl_set_eq:NN \l__nulab_pick_l_tl \Lab@tc }
      }
    \tl_if_blank:VTF \l__nulab_pick_l_tl
      {
        \tl_set:Nn \Lab@tickcol { gray!65 }
        \tl_clear:N \Lab@labkeys
      }
      {
        \tl_set_eq:NN \Lab@tickcol \l__nulab_pick_l_tl
        \tl_set:Nx \Lab@labkeys { text = \exp_not:V \l__nulab_pick_l_tl }
      }
  }
% Non-overlay default for the trace: the call's `trace color`, else nuirred.
\cs_new_protected:Npn \Lab@pickstd
  {
    \tl_if_blank:VTF \l__nulab_plot_tcol_tl
      { \Lab@pickcolors { nuirred } { \c_false_bool } }
      { \exp_args:NV \Lab@pickcolors \l__nulab_plot_tcol_tl { \c_false_bool } }
  }
% A single \irspectrum / \labplot has no panel options of its own.
\cs_new_protected:Npn \Lab@pickstd@single
  {
    \tl_gclear:N \l__nulab_panel_tcol_tl
    \tl_gclear:N \l__nulab_panel_lcol_tl
    \Lab@pickstd
  }
% Overlay: trace i takes entry i of `overlay colors` (cycling, with a
